     4	
     5	`README.md` and `README.pdf` contain the same content.
     6	
     7	# Overview
     8	
     9	This artifact accompanies the paper *To Tag, or Not to Tag: Translating C's
    10	Unions to Rust's Tagged Unions*. The paper introduces static analysis that
    11	identifies tag fields for unions and program transformation that replaces unions
    12	with tagged unions, improving the automatic translation from C to Rust. The
    13	artifact allows for the reproduction of the experimental results presented in
    14	Section 5 of the paper. To use this artifact, Docker support is required, as it
    15	is provided via a Docker image.
    16	
    17	The artifact includes four components: C2Rust, Extern2use, Urcrat, and an
    18	evaluation dataset.
    19	
    20	* C2Rust is a tool that syntactically translates C code to Rust.
    21	* Extern2use replaces extern declarations in C2Rust-generated code with `use`.
    22	* Urcrat is the tool implementing the proposed approach, developed in Rust. It
    23	  analyzes Rust code and transforms it to replace unions with tagged unions.
    24	* The evaluation dataset consists of 35 real-world C programs.
    25	
    26	# Setup
    27	
    28	1. Install Docker by following the instructions at
    29	   <https://docs.docker.com/engine/install>.
    30	2. Start a Docker container by running the following command, which pulls our
    31	   image at <https://hub.docker.com/r/kaistplrg/urcrat>:
    32	
    33	```bash
    34	docker run -it kaistplrg/urcrat:ase2024
    35	```
    36	
    37	# Directory Structure
    38	
    39	Important directories and files:
    40	
    41	```
    42	~ (/home/ubuntu)
    43	|- bin
    44	|- build-scripts
    45	|- c
    46	|- c2rust
    47	|- cp-scripts
    48	|- extern2use
    49	|- fix-scripts
    50	|- gnu-compressed
    51	|- rs
    52	|- rs-resolved
    53	|- rs-transformed
    54	|- test-scripts
    55	|- urcrat
    56	   |- src
    57	      |- may_analysis
    58	          |- mod.rs
    59	      |- must_analysis
    60	          |- analysis.rs
    61	          |- domains.rs
    62	          |- semantics.rs
    63	      |- tag_analysis.rs
    64	```
    65	
    66	* `bin` contains scripts for the experiments.
    67	* `build-scripts` contains scripts to translate each C program to Rust using
    68	  C2Rust and Extern2use. These scripts have already been executed.
    69	* `c2rust` contains the source code of C2Rust.
    70	* `c` contains the source code of each C program.
    71	* `cp-scripts` contains scripts for copying files during the experiments.
    72	* `extern2use` contains the source code of Extern2use.
    73	* `fix-scripts` contains scripts for fixing C or Rust code as described in the
    74	  paper.
    75	* `gnu-compressed` contains the compressed source code of each C program from
    76	  GNU packages.
    77	* `rs` contains the Rust code generated by C2Rust.
    78	* `rs-resolved` contains the Rust code where `extern` declarations have been
    79	  replaced with `use` by Extern2use.
    80	* `rs-transformed` is initially empty and will contain the Rust code transformed
    81	  by Urcrat during the experiments.
    82	* `test-scripts` contains scripts for executing the test suites of the C
    83	  programs.
    84	* `urcrat` contains the source code of Urcrat.
    85	  * `urcrat/src/may_analysis/mod.rs` implements Andersen-style may-points-to
    86	    analysis.
    87	  * `urcrat/src/must_analysis` implements must-points-to analysis.
    88	  * `urcrat/src/must_analysis/analysis.rs` implements work lists and fixed-point
    89	    iterations.
    90	  * `urcrat/src/must_analysis/domains.rs` implements abstract domains.
    91	  * `urcrat/src/must_analysis/semantics.rs` implements abstract transfer
    92	    functions.
    93	  * `urcrat/src/tag_analysis.rs` implements heuristic algorithms that identify
    94	    tag fields and code transformations based on the analysis results.
    95	
    96	Since C2Rust, Extern2use, and Urcrat have already been compiled, they do not
    97	need to be built again to reproduce the results of the paper. However, if one
    98	modifies these tools for future research, they can be rebuilt using the command
    99	`cargo build --release` in each directory.
   100	
   101	# Usage
   102	
   103	Our experimental results are provided in the `results.xlsx` file within the
   104	artifact. Follow the instructions below to reproduce these results. The
   105	described commands will work correctly regardless of the current working
   106	directory in the shell. Follow the exact order of the experiments and do not
   107	skip any steps to obtain the correct results.
   108	
   109	## Benchmark Code Size (Columns B and C)
   110	
   111	Run the following command to measure code sizes:
   112	
   113	```bash
   114	size.sh
   115	```
   116	
   117	This script uses `cloc` to count the lines of C and Rust code. In the Excel
   118	file, columns B and C display the number of lines for C and Rust code,
   119	respectively. These results correspond to the second and third columns of Table
   120	1 in the paper.
   121	
   122	## Analysis and Transformation
   123	
   124	To analyze and transform the benchmark programs using Urcrat, run the following
   125	command:
   126	
   127	```bash
   128	run.sh
   129	```
   130	
   131	This script takes approximately 2 hours to complete. It runs the following
   132	command for each benchmark program directory:
   133	
   134	```bash
   135	urcrat [dir] must -o ~/rs-transformed
   136	```
   137	
   138	The transformed programs will be stored in `~/rs-transformed`.
   139	
   140	For each program, the script outputs 11 numbers:
   141	
   142	* Number of unions
   143	* Number of candidate unions
   144	* Number of identified tag fields
   145	* Time taken
   146	* Number of functions
   147	* Number of analyzed functions
   148	* Number of inserted method calls
   149	* Number of prevented method calls due to `match`
   150	* Number of prevented method calls due to `if-let`
   151	* Number of prevented method calls due to value construction
   152	* Number of lines inserted due to helper method definitions
   153	
   154	Next, we explain how to compare these numbers with the Excel file and the paper.
   155	
   156	### Benchmark Code Size (Columns D and E)
   157	
   158	The first two numbers, the number of unions and the number of candidate unions,
   159	correspond to columns D and E of the Excel file, respectively. These numbers
   160	match the fourth and fifth columns of Table 1 in the paper.
   161	
   162	### RQ1: Precision (Column F)
   163	
   164	The third number, the number of identified tag fields, corresponds to column F
   165	of the Excel file and the sixth column of Table 1 in the paper. The total sum
   166	across all programs is 74, consistent with the description in Section 5.3 of the
   167	paper.
   168	
   169	### RQ3: Scalability (Columns G--J)
   170	
   171	The fourth number, the time to analyze and transform the program (in
   172	milliseconds), corresponds to column G of the Excel file, although the
   173	experimental data may differ from the numbers in the file due to varying
   174	hardware. Its relationship to Rust LOC is illustrated in Figure 2 of the paper.
   175	
   176	The fifth and sixth numbers, the total number of functions and the number of
   177	analyzed functions, correspond to columns H and I of the Excel file. Column J
   178	shows the proportion of analyzed functions. The geometric mean of these
   179	proportions is 4.77%, as noted in Section 5.5 of the paper.
   180	
   181	### RQ4: Code Characteristics (Columns K--S)
   182	
   183	The seventh, eighth, ninth, and tenth numbers correspond to columns K, L, M, and
   184	N of the Excel file. These represent the number of inserted method calls in the
   185	idiomatic transformation and those prevented by inserting `match`, `if-let`, and
   186	value construction instead.
   187	
   188	Note that the numbers for `glpk-5.0` and `uucp-1.07` differ between the file and
   189	the script results. This is due to false negatives in these two programs, which
   190	can be correctly identified after applying manual fixes, as described in Section
   191	5.3 of the paper. To apply the manual fixes, run the following commands:
   192	
   193	```bash
   194	~/fix-scripts/glpk-5.0.sh
   195	~/fix-scripts/uucp-1.07.sh
   196	```
   197	
   198	These commands will create `glpk-5.0-fixed` and `uucp-1.07-fixed` directories
   199	under `~/rs-resolved`. Then, analyze and transform the code by running the
   200	following commands:
   201	
   202	```bash
   203	urcrat ~/rs-resolved/glpk-5.0-fixed must -o ~/rs-transformed
   204	urcrat ~/rs-resolved/uucp-1.07-fixed must -o ~/rs-transformed
   205	```
   206	
   207	The seventh to tenth numbers in their output should match those in columns K--N
   208	of the Excel file.
   209	
   210	Column O shows the sum of columns K--N, representing the number of method calls
   211	that would be inserted by the naive transformation. The average number of method
   212	calls inserted by the naive transformation is 194.6, which matches the
   213	description in Section 5.6 of the paper. Additionally, the averages of columns
   214	K--N are 124.8, 38.3, 15.8, and 15.6, respectively, aligning with the
   215	description in Section 5.6 of the paper.
   216	
   217	Finally, the eleventh number, the number of lines inserted due to helper method
   218	definitions, corresponds to column P of the Excel file. The numbers for
   219	`glpk-5.0` and `uucp-1.07` in the file should match those obtained after
   220	applying the fixes, not those from the initial script run.
   221	
   222	Run the following command to count the lines inserted and deleted due to the
   223	transformation:
   224	
   225	```bash
   226	diff.sh
   227	```
   228	
   229	This command displays the number of lines inserted and deleted for each program,
   230	corresponding to columns Q and R of the Excel file. The number of lines
   231	inserted, excluding those due to helper method definitions, can be calculated by
   232	subtracting column P from column Q, as shown in column S. The average values for
   233	columns P, R, and S are 861.8, 301.8, and 252.4, respectively, matching the
   234	description in Section 5.6 of the paper.
   235	
   236	## Transformed Programs
   237	
   238	### RQ2: Correctness (Column T)
   239	
   240	To type-check the transformed programs, run the following command:
   241	
   242	```bash
   243	check.sh
   244	```
   245	
   246	The script outputs the return code for each type-checking process, where `0`
   247	indicates success. All programs are expected to pass type checking.
   248	
   249	To execute the test suite for each program, directories with multiple
   250	conflicting `main` functions need to be duplicated. To do this, run the
   251	following command:
   252	
   253	```bash
   254	prepare.sh
   255	```
   256	
   257	Next, compile the programs by running the following command:
   258	
   259	```bash
   260	build.sh
   261	```
   262	
   263	Finally, run the test suites with the following command:
   264	
   265	```bash
   266	test.sh
   267	```
   268	
   269	The script will output the return code for each test, where `0` indicates
   270	success. Out of 23 programs with test suites, 17 are expected to pass, except
   271	for `gawk-5.2.2`, `grep-3.11`, `make-4.4.1`, `minilisp`, `twemproxy`, and
   272	`wget-1.21.4`. These results correspond to column T of the Excel file and
   273	align with the description in Section 5.4 of the paper. Four programs,
   274	`grep-3.11`, `make-4.4.1`, `minilisp`, and `wget-1.21.4`, require small manual
   275	fixes, as described in Section 5.4 of the paper, to run correctly after the
   276	transformation.
   277	
   278	To apply manual fixes, transform, and run the test suite for each program,
   279	follow the below steps. The test suites are now expected to succeed.
   280	
   281	For `grep-3.11`:
   282	
   283	```bash
   284	~/fix-scripts/grep-3.11.sh
   285	urcrat ~/rs-resolved/grep-3.11-fixed must -o ~/rs-transformed
   286	cd ~/rs-transformed/grep-3.11-fixed && RUSTFLAGS=-Awarnings cargo build --release
   287	~/cp-scripts/grep-3.11.sh ~/rs-transformed -fixed && ~/test-scripts/grep-3.11.sh
   288	```
   289	
   290	For `make-4.4.1`:
   291	
   292	```bash
   293	~/fix-scripts/make-4.4.1.sh
   294	urcrat ~/rs-resolved/make-4.4.1-fixed must -o ~/rs-transformed
   295	cd ~/rs-transformed/make-4.4.1-fixed && RUSTFLAGS=-Awarnings cargo build --release
   296	~/cp-scripts/make-4.4.1.sh ~/rs-transformed -fixed && ~/test-scripts/make-4.4.1.sh
   297	```
   298	
   299	For `minilisp`:
   300	
   301	```bash
   302	~/fix-scripts/minilisp.sh
   303	urcrat ~/rs-resolved/minilisp-fixed must -o ~/rs-transformed
   304	cd ~/rs-transformed/minilisp-fixed && RUSTFLAGS=-Awarnings cargo build --release
   305	~/cp-scripts/minilisp.sh ~/rs-transformed -fixed && ~/test-scripts/minilisp.sh
   306	```
   307	
   308	For `wget-1.21.4`:
   309	
   310	```bash
   311	~/fix-scripts/wget-1.21.4.sh
   312	urcrat ~/rs-resolved/wget-1.21.4-fixed must -o ~/rs-transformed
   313	cd ~/rs-transformed/wget-1.21.4-fixed && RUSTFLAGS=-Awarnings cargo build --release
   314	~/cp-scripts/wget-1.21.4.sh ~/rs-transformed -fixed && ~/test-scripts/wget-1.21.4.sh
   315	```
   316	
   317	### RQ5: Impact on Performance (Columns U--W)
   318	
   319	To measure the average execution time of the test suite across 50 runs for each
   320	program before the transformation, run the following command:
   321	
   322	```bash
   323	performance.sh ~/rs-resolved 50
   324	```
   325	
   326	These results correspond to column U of the Excel file, although the
   327	experimental data may differ from the numbers in the file due to varying
   328	hardware.
   329	
   330	Next, measure the execution times for the programs after the transformation by
   331	running the following command:
   332	
   333	```bash
   334	performance.sh ~/rs-transformed 50 -fixed
   335	```
   336	
   337	These results correspond to column V of the Excel file. Column W shows the
   338	performance difference, calculated by dividing column V by column U. The
   339	geometric mean of these values is 100.01%, consistent with the description in
   340	Section 5.7 of the paper.
   341	


